  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/2cb9e5fc9fb0e5b1ea95406ba9961cc176a9c637f354b1e8ff2d574cbb1261d6.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?",
  "worked_solution": "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only.",
